WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.650
- [Narrator] The New Hampshire primary

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.420
is just around the corner.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.790
- This is potentially a pivotal moment

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.500
in the Republican primary.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.490
- [Narrator] For Nikki Haley,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.190
New Hampshire could be
her best and last chance

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.800
to persuade voters that
she's a better alternative

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.120
to Donald Trump.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:15.953
Here's why.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.610
The first primary of
the 2024 election season

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.620
is now a one-on-one battle

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.600
after Florida governor Ron DeSantis

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.840
suspended his presidential bid
two days before the election

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.390
and endorsed former
president Donald Trump.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.120
- I can't ask our supporters
to volunteer their time

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.830
and donate their resources

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.350
if we don't have a clear path to victory.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.630
- [Narrator] The New Hampshire
primary comes off the heels

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.970
of the Republican caucuses in Iowa,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.400
where Trump won by a
record breaking margin,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.950
all but cementing his
position as the front runner

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.930
for the party's nomination.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.350
- We wanna thank the great people of Iowa.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.450
Thank you. We love you all.
(crowd cheering)

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.890
- [Narrator] But unlike Iowa,

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.200
New Hampshire has a more
moderate electorate,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.880
and large numbers of independents

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.270
are allowed to vote in
the state's GOP primary.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.460
- New Hampshire's much
more independent-minded,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.690
much more secular.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:07.920
This has always been a state

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.050
where the electorate
was much more friendly

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.540
to the appeal of someone like Nikki Haley,

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.940
who combines a sort of conservative stance

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.890
on taxes and spending,

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.800
with conservative
positions on foreign policy

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.080
and a pitch that's about electability.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.460
There's a substantial block

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.560
of Democratic-leaning independents,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.570
who might like to take the nomination away

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:28.800
from Donald Trump.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.800
There's a substantial block
of moderate conservatives

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.050
or conservative-leaning independents.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.930
All of these moderate
voters are a potential asset

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.883
for a candidate like Nikki Haley.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.670
- [Narrator] With
DeSantis out of the race,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.040
Haley has the opportunity
to present herself

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.310
as the better and now
only alternative to Trump.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.750
- It has been the conventional wisdom

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.000
that the only way Trump could be beaten

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.910
is if he were one-on-one
with a single alternative,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.100
giving voters that binary choice,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.500
either it's Trump or it's not.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.900
- Do we want more of the same

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.120
or do you want something different?

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.400
And more of the same isn't just Joe Biden,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.920
more of the same is also Donald Trump.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.200
- [Narrator] Haley believes
she already has momentum

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.450
going into the New Hampshire primary.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.000
- She has the endorsement
of the very popular

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.730
Republican governor of New
Hampshire, Chris Sununu,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.340
who's been very outspoken against Trump

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.200
and what he represents.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.060
- [Narrator] Haley is
also likely to scoop up

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.280
some supporters of former
New Jersey governor,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.620
Chris Christie, an avid Trump critic

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.910
who dropped out of the race

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.940
just days before the Iowa caucuses.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.210
However, Trump remains dominant
in polls in New Hampshire

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.360
and the states to follow
for future primaries.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.440
With DeSantis endorsing Trump
after suspending his campaign,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.510
the support he was
drawing in New Hampshire

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.810
will likely go to Trump,
widening his lead.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.540
Another decisive victory could
make the former president

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.940
nearly impossible to stop.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.530
- From the perspective
of the Trump campaign,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:56.820
this is already over.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.120
You have a lot of Trump's
allies and campaign officials

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.510
saying, "It's time to unite
the Republican Party."

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.020
Haley has tried to toe
this very delicate line of,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.540
on the one hand, articulating
what she believes

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.280
are valid criticisms of Trump,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.930
while not angering the voters

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.780
who still think that
Trump was a good president

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.790
and like him in a lot of ways.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.150
And so for her, it's really
New Hampshire that's do or die.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.620
She needs to either win

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.990
or show that she can come
close to Donald Trump

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.450
to give her a reason
to keep going forward.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.283
- [Narrator] It's still early

00:03:28.283 --> 00:03:30.750
in the 2024 presidential campaign season,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.870
with many more state
primaries and caucuses to go

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.940
ahead of November's election.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.860
But New Hampshire will likely set the tone

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.990
for another major upcoming task,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.820
the South Carolina primary.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.340
- Trump looks very strong
throughout the South,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.720
including in South Carolina.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.640
We've seen him get huge reception

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.590
when he's gone down there to campaign.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.990
So although it is Nikki
Haley's home state,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.030
where she was a popular governor,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.410
it's viewed as an uphill battle

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.910
for all the non-Trump candidates.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.650
If Haley is able to come close

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.210
or defeat Trump in New Hampshire,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.820
she really looks like
a much more of a force

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.170
in this campaign,

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.540
and it starts to look like a real contest.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.420
Now, she still has an uphill
battle in South Carolina,

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.016
but at least it looks
like an actual battle.

00:04:12.016 --> 00:04:14.599
(gentle music)

